---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/thermodynamics-and-enthalpy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every reaction trades energy, and two questions decide its fate: <strong>how much</strong>
    (enthalpy, ΔH) and <strong>which way does it want to go</strong> (free energy, ΔG). Burning
    releases; melting absorbs. But releases don't automatically happen — petrol sits safely in a
    tank — because a reaction must also climb an <strong>activation-energy hill</strong> before
    it can descend.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two reaction paths — both may need a push uphill before settling lower (or higher) than they started" widget="plotter" config={enthalpyCfg} />

  <p>
    The exothermic path ends below its start (ΔH negative — heat released); the endothermic path
    ends above (ΔH positive — heat absorbed, like photosynthesis). Either can proceed if the
    activation hill is climbed — by heat, or by a catalyst lowering the hill without moving the
    endpoints.
  </p>

  <h2>Gibbs free energy: the real referee</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.</strong> A reaction wins when it lowers free energy: release heat (ΔH &lt; 0) and/or increase disorder (ΔS > 0). Ice melting above 0 °C wins on entropy despite absorbing heat.</li>
    <li><strong>Spontaneous ≠ fast.</strong> ΔG says nothing about speed. Diamond → graphite is spontaneous — over geological timescales. Kinetics (next lesson) owns speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Bond arithmetic:</strong> ΔH ≈ (bonds broken) − (bonds formed). Breaking costs energy; forming pays it back. Fuels are molecules whose products (CO₂, H₂O) hold much stronger bonds — the difference is the flame.</li>
    <li><strong>Hess's law:</strong> enthalpy is path-independent — add reaction steps like algebra. The trick that lets chemists measure unmeasurable reactions indirectly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gibbs (1873); Hess (1840); Atkins, Physical Chemistry.</p>
</TopicPage>
